---
import TimerPage from '@/layouts/TimerPage.astro';
import Section from '@/components/Section.astro';
import { faqs } from '@/data/faqs';
import { relatedTimers, relatedPosts } from '@/data/related';
import { defaultConfigs } from '@/engine/presets';
---

<TimerPage
  title="EMOM Timer – Free Every Minute On the Minute Timer"
  description="Set an EMOM timer with intervals from 15 seconds to five minutes. Choose your total duration and see how each work period leaves time for recovery."
  path="/emom"
  h1="EMOM timer"
  lede="This EMOM timer beeps at the start of every minute, on the minute. Set the interval and the total time, then work to the beep."
  config={defaultConfigs.emom}
  faq={faqs['/emom']}
  related={relatedTimers['/emom']!}
  posts={relatedPosts['/emom']}
  crumbName="EMOM timer"
>
  <Section title="What is an EMOM workout?" flush>
    <p>
      EMOM means every minute on the minute. You begin a planned set at each minute's start and
      recover in the time left. It is a way to organise exercises, with predictable starts and a
      visible recovery budget.
    </p>
    <p>
      It can suit a familiar task with a clear repetition target. If a task needs more time to learn
      or recover from, a longer interval or another structure may fit better. The{' '}
      <a href="/blog/emom-workouts-explained">EMOM explainer</a> shows when this format is useful
      and how it differs from fixed work/rest periods.
    </p>
  </Section>
  <Section title="The minute includes your recovery">
    <p>
      Begin your planned set when the minute starts. If the set and reset take 25 seconds, you have
      35 seconds before the next start. If they take 50 seconds, only ten remain. The clock fixes
      start times, not how much recovery you get.
    </p>
    <p>
      Choose movements, repetitions and effort from an appropriate plan. If recovery disappears,
      adjust or stop rather than rushing. The <a href="/blog/emom-workouts-explained">EMOM guide</a>{' '}
      compares this with fixed work/rest intervals.
    </p>
  </Section>
  <Section title="Check how the blocks fit">
    <p>
      Interval length uses seconds, from 15 to 300. Set 120 for every two minutes, or 90 for every
      ninety seconds. Total minutes sets the end of the whole session, from 1 to 99 minutes.
    </p>
    <p>
      The last block is shortened when the total does not divide evenly. Ten minutes with
      ninety-second blocks gives six full blocks and one thirty-second block. Choose nine or twelve
      minutes if you want only complete ninety-second blocks.
    </p>
  </Section>
  <Section title="Prepare the sequence">
    <p>
      The timer counts blocks but does not announce exercise names. Write down any rotation first. A
      twelve-minute A/B/C rotation contains four sets of each task. Start begins the first block
      immediately.
    </p>
    <p>
      Keep the page visible and test volume. Warning ticks occur in the last three seconds, then a
      cue marks the next block. A hidden page may miss sounds. Use <a href="/interval">Interval</a>{' '}
      for fixed work/rest pairs or <a href="/tabata">Tabata</a> for the fixed 20/10 pattern.
    </p>
  </Section>
</TimerPage>
